From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 19:50:46 +0200
Subject: dt-bindings: phy: add Intel GRX500 USB PHY

Document the binding the intel,grx500-usb-phy driver has been reading
since it was added. Nothing here is new: the properties are the ones the
driver already parses and the two boards already spell, written down.

Both instances appear as examples on purpose. Everything that
distinguishes them is a property value -- register window, gate clock,
reset bit, chip-top offset -- so a binding that showed one instance would
not show that any of it is parameterised. Both are in use: the FRITZ!Box
7590 enables both controllers, the FRITZ!Box 7560 only the second.

Four notes on things a reader may reasonably question, none of which the
binding itself is the place for.

The resets property refers to the second RCU reset bank, which is a
sibling reset-controller node inside the RCU with descending reg entries
(<0x48 4>, <0x24 4>) and two cells. That is not an invention: it is the
example in Documentation/devicetree/bindings/mips/lantiq/rcu.txt, which
shows the same node beside the first bank, and lantiq,reset.yaml
validates each node on its own with no ordering constraint on reg. The
same file documents the xRX200's two USB PHYs on bank 1 bits 4 and 5,
with configuration words at +0x18 and +0x34 -- the pairing this SoC
keeps, with the word moved into the chip-top syscon.

The polarity properties are the vendor's, with a vendor prefix added.
AVM's driver reads them unprefixed as invert-tx-polarity and
invert-rx-polarity, which are not generic properties and should not look
like ones; nothing outside that tree uses either spelling, so renaming
them costs no compatibility.

The driver's Kconfig entry uses select RESET_LANTIQ rather than
depends on. RESET_LANTIQ has no prompt outside COMPILE_TEST, so a
depends on would make the driver unselectable on the platform that needs
it, which is the case select exists for.

The three unconditional 100 ms settling delays in the driver -- after
the endian write, between the gate clock and the reset release, and
between that and VBUS -- are the vendor's values reproduced as-is. The
comment above them in drivers/phy/intel/phy-intel-grx500-usb.c says why:
this SoC has no publicly available hardware reference manual, nothing in
the vendor sources says what is being waited for, and its kernel is the
only one known to bring the block up. They are a driver detail and do
not belong in a binding.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/phy/intel,grx500-usb-phy.yaml b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/phy/intel,grx500-usb-phy.yaml
new file mode 100644
index 000000000000..06e872d8e47d
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Documentation/devicetree/bindings/phy/intel,grx500-usb-phy.yaml
@@ -0,0 +1,115 @@
+# SPDX-License-Identifier: (GPL-2.0-only OR BSD-2-Clause)
+%YAML 1.2
+---
+$id: http://devicetree.org/schemas/phy/intel,grx500-usb-phy.yaml#
+$schema: http://devicetree.org/meta-schemas/core.yaml#
+
+title: Intel GRX500 USB PHY
+
+maintainers:
+  - Grische <github@grische.xyz>
+
+description: |
+  The USB PHY feeding one of the two on-chip Synopsys DesignWare USB3
+  controllers of the Intel MIPS xRX500 (GRX500) SoC family.
+
+  Each controller has a PHY of its own, and the two instances differ only in
+  the values of the properties below: a register window, a gate clock, a bit
+  in the second RCU reset bank, and the offset of an AXI configuration word
+  in the chip-top syscon.
+
+  The PHY also has an over-current interrupt, which is not described here
+  because no board wires over-current detection to the pads that feed it.
+
+properties:
+  compatible:
+    const: intel,grx500-usb-phy
+
+  reg:
+    maxItems: 1
+
+  clocks:
+    maxItems: 1
+    description: The PHY's gate clock.
+
+  resets:
+    maxItems: 1
+
+  reset-names:
+    items:
+      - const: phy
+
+  intel,syscon:
+    $ref: /schemas/types.yaml#/definitions/phandle-array
+    items:
+      - items:
+          - description: phandle to the chip-top syscon
+          - description: >
+              byte offset within it of the AXI configuration word belonging
+              to the controller this PHY feeds
+    description:
+      The AXI configuration word selects the byte order of each of the DWC3
+      core's two AXI master ports. It sits in the chip-top syscon rather
+      than in the PHY's own register window, at a different offset per
+      controller.
+
+  vbus-supply:
+    description:
+      Port power for the controller this PHY feeds. Optional, since port
+      power is not always software controlled. Both PHYs may name the same
+      regulator on a board that switches its ports together.
+
+  intel,invert-tx-polarity:
+    type: boolean
+    description:
+      Invert the polarity of the SuperSpeed transmit pair, for a board that
+      routes it crossed.
+
+  intel,invert-rx-polarity:
+    type: boolean
+    description:
+      Invert the polarity of the SuperSpeed receive pair, for a board that
+      routes it crossed.
+
+  "#phy-cells":
+    const: 0
+
+required:
+  - compatible
+  - reg
+  - clocks
+  - resets
+  - reset-names
+  - intel,syscon
+  - "#phy-cells"
+
+additionalProperties: false
+
+examples:
+  - |
+    #include <dt-bindings/clock/intel,grx500-clk.h>
+
+    phy@1a400000 {
+        compatible = "intel,grx500-usb-phy";
+        reg = <0x1a400000 0x10000>;
+        clocks = <&cgu GCLK_USB0>;
+        resets = <&rcu_reset1 4 4>;
+        reset-names = "phy";
+        intel,syscon = <&chiptop 0x18>;
+        vbus-supply = <&usb_vbus>;
+        #phy-cells = <0>;
+    };
+
+  - |
+    #include <dt-bindings/clock/intel,grx500-clk.h>
+
+    phy@1a600000 {
+        compatible = "intel,grx500-usb-phy";
+        reg = <0x1a600000 0x10000>;
+        clocks = <&cgu GCLK_USB1>;
+        resets = <&rcu_reset1 5 5>;
+        reset-names = "phy";
+        intel,syscon = <&chiptop 0x34>;
+        vbus-supply = <&usb_vbus>;
+        #phy-cells = <0>;
+    };
